WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.490
Two lights repeat at different speeds.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.330
One flashes every four seconds and the other every six.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.300
We can ask when they first flash

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:09.770
together after starting.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.860
We can also ask how much time separates one meeting

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:13.650
from the next.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.710
Twenty-four is a multiple of both four and six.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.790
A smaller common multiple may also work.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.130
First mark where each light starts.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.910
Then add its own period each time.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.940
A timeline shows both the starting positions and

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:29.850
the repeated jumps.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.810
Both lights flash at time zero.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.850
One repeats every four seconds.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.070
The other repeats every six seconds.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.050
When do they next flash together?

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.770
List a few times for each light, and line up the lists.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:49.200
The first light flashes at four, eight,

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:50.300
twelve and sixteen.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.640
The second flashes at six, twelve and eighteen.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.840
Their first positive meeting is at twelve seconds.

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:00.520
Twelve is the lowest common multiple of four and six.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:04.070
A gap between meetings has to be a multiple of both

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:07.460
periods, so the next meeting is twelve seconds later.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.670
Let the second light start at time two.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.310
Its times are two, eight, fourteen and twenty.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.330
The first light's times stay on the multiples of four.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.660
The first meeting moves to eight seconds, and the next

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:20.330
is at twenty.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:22.970
The starting offset changed the first meeting.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.430
The twelve-second gap stayed the same.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.510
Now start the second light at time one.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.630
Its jumps of six keep all its times odd.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:35.620
Starting from zero and moving in jumps of four keeps the

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:36.570
other times even.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:40.020
An odd time and an even time cannot match, so these two

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.430
lights never flash together.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:45.070
When meetings are possible, the lowest common multiple

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:46.700
gives the gap between them.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:49.010
The starting positions decide when the

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:50.160
first meeting happens.

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:53.970
In your notebook, one bus leaves every eight minutes and

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:55.020
another every twelve.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:57.040
They both leave at nine o'clock.

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:58.820
When do they next leave together?
